  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Marked Out Before the Dig",
    "overview": "The site is a residential lawn the morning a crew arrives: Dig Safe locate paint sprayed across New England grass, wet loam where the ground is open, granite curb sections laid in between. The page alternates two grounds, dark loam and speckled granite, and every accent is a material a crew actually uses: machine yellow for anything you press, survey-flag pink for the form field you are filling in, and the four utility locate colors (water blue, gas yellow, sewer green, electric red) as sprayed marks under headings and as the pipes buried in the hero lawn. Photos get white-lining, the dashed paint perimeter crews spray around a dig area.\n\nLettering is heavy, uppercase, tightly tracked system type, set large. The density is low and the targets are big because the visitor is a homeowner on a phone, usually inside Facebook's in-app browser. The signature is physical: the hero lawn is a canvas you dig into with a finger to uncover the colored pipes beneath each mark, and grass fringes sway along every top edge where a soil ground meets the section above it. Both react to the pointer and rest when off-screen.\n\nThe build rejects the stock contractor page: no stock excavator photography, no hazard stripes, no row of three icon cards.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Two alternating grounds: loam brown (dark) and granite gray with real mica-and-quartz speckle (light).",
      "Accent colors are site materials with fixed jobs, never decoration.",
      "Heavy uppercase system lettering, tight negative tracking, display sizes driven by viewport width.",
      "Spray-paint texture (an SVG turbulence filter) on every painted element: heading marks, step numerals, white-lining.",
      "Living ground: dig-to-reveal lawn canvas plus grass fringes, both tuned through `CFG` constants at the top of their JS modules, both honoring reduced motion."
